\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.0.1}{\ignorespaces The running array \(A\) (top), its BIT \(T\) (middle, \(T[i]=\) sum of \(A\) over the green block below it), and the responsibility table (bottom). Build checks reused everywhere: \(T[4]=3{+}2{+}4{+}5{=}14\), \(T[2]=3{+}2{=}5\), \(T[8]=14{+}1{+}1{+}5{+}3{=}24\) (CSES~1648's first sample answer), \(T[12]=7{+}0{+}2{+}4{=}13\), \(T[14]=A[13]{+}A[14]=1{+}6{=}7\), and \(T[16]=\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ A=49\). The red arrows show one \emph  {query} walk: \(6\to 4\to 0\) drops the lowest set bit each step and visits \(T[6],T[4]\) covering \([5,6]\uplus [1,4]=[1,6]\) -- disjoint blocks, no double counting. The mirror walk (update \(A_3{+}3\)) climbs \(3\to 4\to 8\to 16\), touching every interval that contains slot~3; both walks are \S  \ref {sec:c14-core}'s opening trace.}}{1081}{figure.caption.188}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c14-array}{{18.0.1}{1081}{The running array \(A\) (top), its BIT \(T\) (middle, \(T[i]=\) sum of \(A\) over the green block below it), and the responsibility table (bottom). Build checks reused everywhere: \(T[4]=3{+}2{+}4{+}5{=}14\), \(T[2]=3{+}2{=}5\), \(T[8]=14{+}1{+}1{+}5{+}3{=}24\) (CSES~1648's first sample answer), \(T[12]=7{+}0{+}2{+}4{=}13\), \(T[14]=A[13]{+}A[14]=1{+}6{=}7\), and \(T[16]=\sum A=49\). The red arrows show one \emph {query} walk: \(6\to 4\to 0\) drops the lowest set bit each step and visits \(T[6],T[4]\) covering \([5,6]\uplus [1,4]=[1,6]\) -- disjoint blocks, no double counting. The mirror walk (update \(A_3{+}3\)) climbs \(3\to 4\to 8\to 16\), touching every interval that contains slot~3; both walks are \secref {c14-core}'s opening trace}{figure.caption.188}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.0.2}{\ignorespaces The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation. Rows 1--2 are the machine; row~3 is a change of \emph  {what you store}; row~4 is a change of \emph  {algebra} (two trees, one identity); row~5 runs the tree \emph  {backwards} (search instead of sum); row~6 is a change of \emph  {schedule} -- queries arrive sorted, not online. Every solved problem sits on exactly one row: CSES~1648 on rows~1--2, CSES~1651 on row~3, the two-BIT worked example on row~4, CF~61E and CSES~1143 on row~5, CF~383C on row~3-plus-Ch.5, and the Summit CF~1181D spanning rows~5--6.}}{1082}{figure.caption.189}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c14-taxonomy}{{18.0.2}{1082}{The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation. Rows 1--2 are the machine; row~3 is a change of \emph {what you store}; row~4 is a change of \emph {algebra} (two trees, one identity); row~5 runs the tree \emph {backwards} (search instead of sum); row~6 is a change of \emph {schedule} -- queries arrive sorted, not online. Every solved problem sits on exactly one row: CSES~1648 on rows~1--2, CSES~1651 on row~3, the two-BIT worked example on row~4, CF~61E and CSES~1143 on row~5, CF~383C on row~3-plus-Ch.5, and the Summit CF~1181D spanning rows~5--6}{figure.caption.189}{}}
